Why the Blind 75 Matters
In 2018, a Meta (then Facebook) tech lead named Yangshun Tay posted a curated list of 75 LeetCode problems on the anonymous forum Blind. His goal was simple: help engineers prepare efficiently without grinding through thousands of problems.

The list gained traction because it worked. Engineers who studied these problems reported seeing similar patterns in their actual interviews. This isn’t because companies copy from the Blind 75, but because these problems represent fundamental programming patterns that naturally appear in technical assessments.

According to Sean Prashad’s LeetCode Patterns, which tracks problem frequencies across companies, certain problems like Two Sum, Valid Parentheses, and Merge Two Sorted Lists appear consistently in interview reports.

Understanding Problem Patterns (Not Memorizing Solutions)
The value isn’t in memorizing these specific problems. Companies know these lists exist and often modify problems or create variations. The value is in understanding the underlying patterns.

Frequently Reported Patterns
Based on community-reported experiences compiled by Tech Interview Handbook:

Arrays & Hashing

Two Sum and its variations (3Sum, 4Sum)
Sliding window problems (substrings, subarrays)
Two-pointer techniques
Trees & Graphs

Tree traversals (DFS, BFS)
Graph search (islands, connected components)
Path-finding problems
Dynamic Programming

Fibonacci-style problems (climbing stairs, house robber)
Grid traversal (unique paths)
String/sequence problems (longest common subsequence)
These patterns reportedly appear across different companies, though the exact problems vary.

